// The answers layer: the part of this site an answer engine can actually quote.
//
// WHY THIS EXISTS
// The site is eleven thousand pages of calculators. A calculator is useless to ChatGPT,
// Perplexity, Google's AI Overviews or anything else that answers a question by quoting a
// source: there is no sentence on the page to lift. So the site had no way of being cited,
// which is the only way those systems send traffic.
//
// What it does have, and almost nobody else does, is rules/: 122 verified rules, each with
// the article it comes from, the official URL, and the date a human last read it. That is
// exactly the shape of thing an answer engine prefers to cite. It was sitting in JSON,
// rendered nowhere.
//
// This turns it into questions. One page per question, the answer in the first sentence,
// the article underneath it, the date it was checked, and a link to the tool that does the
// arithmetic. Short on purpose: the job is to be quotable, not to be long.
//
// EVERY ANSWER IS TIED TO VERIFIED RULES
// `rules` names the ids. A build gate refuses any answer whose rules are missing or are not
// status "verified", so the answers layer cannot drift from the sourced base underneath it.

